chte","plural"],["seichten","seichtes","genitive"]]},"topf":{"entries":[["topf",null,"/tɔpf/","From Middle High German topf, from Old High German *topf, from Proto-West Germanic *dopp, from Proto-Germanic *duppaz (“hollow, bowl”), from Proto-Indo-European *dʰewb- (“deep, hollow”). Cognate with Dutch dop (“little cup, thimble”), Low German Dopp (“shell, pod, bowl”), Saterland Frisian Dop (“shell, pod, bowl”), English dop (“cup in which a diamond is cut”).\nBoth in historic sources and modern dialects, the word is almost entirely restricted to East Central German (generally as Topp, in the very south as Topf). West Central German only shows a neuter derivative in Middle High German düppen (whence e.g. Luxembourgish Dëppen). Nevertheless it must have existed in Old High German as the a-mutation assures inheritance. Beginning with Luther's Bible it established itself as the general standard term for “pot”.",{"g":"m","decl":"||/e|es/s|3öpfe|3öpfe|3öpfen|3öpfe"}]],"senses":[[11740,"topf","noun","pot (flat-bottomed, open-topped vessel, especially for cooking food)",0,"m"],[11741,"topf","noun","saucepan (deep cooking vessel with one handle)",1,"m"]],"related_forms":[[15783,"topf","töpfern","derived"],[15784,"topf","töpfer","derived"],[15785,"topf","topfblume","related"],[15786,"topf","topfdeckel","related"],[15787,"topf","topfgriff","related"],[15788,"topf","topfhenkel","related"],[15789,"topf","topflappen","related"],[15790,"topf","topfschlagen","related"],[15791,"topf","zerdeppern","related"]]},"werkseigener":{"entries":[["werkseigener",null,null,null]],"senses":[[721161,"werkseigener","adj","inflection of werkseigen:",0],[721162,"werkseigener","adj","strong/mixed nominative masculine singular",1],[721163,"werkseigener","adj","strong genitive/dative feminine singular",2],[721164,"werkseigener","adj","strong genitive plural",3]],"inflections":[["werkseigener","werkseigen","masculine,nominative,singular,strong"]]},"neuenburgischstes":{"inflections":[["neuenburgischstes","ne